WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.400 --> 00:00:07.420
A desert iguana basking on a rock can end up warmer than you are.

2
00:00:08.260 --> 00:00:09.560
So what does cold-blooded mean?

3
00:00:10.480 --> 00:00:14.820
Body temperature could depend on the heat an animal makes, the heat it loses, or a

4
00:00:14.820 --> 00:00:20.300
controller. Metabolic rate is energy used per unit of time, and nearly all of it

5
00:00:20.300 --> 00:00:21.300
ends up as heat.

6
00:00:22.133 --> 00:00:24.113
Mammals and birds are endotherms.

7
00:00:24.953 --> 00:00:29.073
They warm themselves mainly with heat from their own metabolism, held in by

8
00:00:29.073 --> 00:00:34.113
insulation. Ectotherms, like the desert iguana, take their temperature mainly from

9
00:00:34.113 --> 00:00:36.953
outside, such as the sun or a warm rock.

10
00:00:37.913 --> 00:00:39.713
Ectotherm does not mean a cold animal.

11
00:00:40.717 --> 00:00:42.777
Heat leaves the body by four routes.

12
00:00:44.017 --> 00:00:47.817
Radiation is emission and absorption of electromagnetic energy.

13
00:00:48.817 --> 00:00:51.077
Conduction passes heat through direct contact.

14
00:00:52.037 --> 00:00:54.397
Convection is transfer by moving air or water.

15
00:00:55.397 --> 00:00:58.497
Evaporation carries heat away as water turns to vapour.

16
00:00:59.357 --> 00:01:00.897
The first three run either way.

17
00:01:01.757 --> 00:01:04.617
Evaporation only removes heat, and it costs water.

18
00:01:04.617 --> 00:01:05.017
heat away as water turns to vapor.

19
00:01:05.017 --> 00:01:05.650
The second run is

20
00:01:05.650 --> 00:01:09.750
Plot an endotherm's resting metabolic rate against air temperature.

21
00:01:10.690 --> 00:01:15.170
Inside the thermoneutral zone, the line is flat, at basal metabolic rate.

22
00:01:16.230 --> 00:01:18.290
Below the lower critical temperature, it climbs.

23
00:01:19.250 --> 00:01:23.970
Above the upper critical temperature, it rises again, as panting and sweating take

24
00:01:23.970 --> 00:01:29.990
work. In 1950, Scholander and Irving found that thick-furred arctic mammals stay

25
00:01:29.990 --> 00:01:32.590
flat into much colder air than tropical ones.

26
00:01:33.583 --> 00:01:38.783
If body temperature and conductance stay roughly constant, the steepness of the cold

27
00:01:38.783 --> 00:01:43.183
limb equals thermal conductance, how readily heat leaks out per degree of

28
00:01:43.183 --> 00:01:46.403
difference. These numbers are invented for practice.

29
00:01:47.283 --> 00:01:55.943
A small endotherm holds 37 degrees C, with a conductance of 0.020 W per K. At 27

30
00:01:55.943 --> 00:02:04.283
degrees, the gap is 10 K, so it makes 0.20 W. At 17 degrees, the gap is 20 K, so it

31
00:02:04.283 --> 00:02:10.823
makes 0.40 W. Fluff the fur until conductance halves, and that cost drops back to

32
00:02:10.823 --> 00:02:12.283
0.20 W.

33
00:02:13.333 --> 00:02:16.413
A set point is the value a control system defends.

34
00:02:17.393 --> 00:02:18.613
In fever, it is raised.

35
00:02:19.513 --> 00:02:23.213
In torpor, a bird or mammal's body temperature drops under control.

36
00:02:24.113 --> 00:02:30.293
In hibernating American black bears, metabolism fell to 25% % of basal while body

37
00:02:30.293 --> 00:02:36.473
temperature stayed between 30 and 36 degrees C. Cooling alone does not explain that.

38
00:02:37.333 --> 00:02:42.893
Keep one rule. For an endotherm at steady state, heat made equals heat lost, and the

39
00:02:42.893 --> 00:02:46.093
temperature it defends can shift, as in fever or torpor.
